    {
      "id": "tGHLu1H73QZjqN2AP3POX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -6310.465109438807,
      "y": 2229.0013592355626,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "6Hb1xWTAmXpEFbaexLb1c",
        "5akRBRWfWLCEWaUF_0qjy"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVa4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1838069178,
      "version": 2767,
      "versionNonce": 1303155664,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789421702582,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Static post-training quantization\n\n\nWe observe that the weights of LLMs are not equally important: there is a small fraction of salient\nweights that are much more important for LLMs’ performance compared to others. Skipping the\nquantization of these salient weights can help mitigate the performance degradation\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTo verify the idea, we benchmark the performance of quantized LLMs when skipping part of the weight\nchannels. Keeping a small fraction of weights (0.1%-1%) in FP16 is only effective when we select the\nimportant weights in FP16 by looking at activation distribution instead of weight distribution. We\nhighlight results with a decent perplexity in green.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Static post-training quantization\n\n\nWe observe that the weights of LLMs are not equally important: there is a small fraction of salient weights that are much more important for LLMs’ performance compared to others. Skipping the quantization of these salient weights can help mitigate the performance degradation\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTo verify the idea, we benchmark the performance of quantized LLMs when skipping part of the weight channels. Keeping a small fraction of weights (0.1%-1%) in FP16 is only effective when we select the important weights in FP16 by looking at activation distribution instead of weight distribution. We highlight results with a decent perplexity in green.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
