      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "vwqxBbsVT5g7QbEktic1s",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3942.8614371303206,
      "y": 2307.492351132645,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "JxM1W5XFElZVzPQVfRQl0",
        "jlfZ8rE6zfya4Cjd_rDFa"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVf1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2095305530,
      "version": 6906,
      "versionNonce": 350231504,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789421757274,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "GGUF started as a quantization technique originally thought for 1 batch inference.\n\nAlso, differently frmo what we saw, there is NO OFFICIAL PAPER NOR DOCUMENTATION.\n\nThe project had \"humble\" origins: according to the creator of the library, ggeranov, everything started\nfrom a hacking evening, while trying to run a LLaMA model using 4-bit integer quantization on a MacBook.\nIn other words, the idea was initially to run inference of local models with minimal hardware.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    Since the creation of llamacpp, the libary using GGUF models, (first commit dated 5 March 2023),\nmany things have changed. It is now one of the most widely used quantization formats, alongside GPTQ\nand AWQ. The project has over +120k stars on GitHub, and it not only a quantization method but also:\n- tensor library for machine learning (a true alternative to pytorch written C and C++)\n- LLM inference engine mostly targeting CPUs\n- binary file format for storing quantized models",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "GGUF started as a quantization technique originally thought for 1 batch inference.\n\nAlso, differently frmo what we saw, there is NO OFFICIAL PAPER NOR DOCUMENTATION.\n\nThe project had \"humble\" origins: according to the creator of the library, ggeranov, everything started from a hacking evening, while trying to run a LLaMA model using 4-bit integer quantization on a MacBook. In other words, the idea was initially to run inference of local models with minimal hardware.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    Since the creation of llamacpp, the libary using GGUF models, (first commit dated 5 March 2023), many things have changed. It is now one of the most widely used quantization formats, alongside GPTQ and AWQ. The project has over +120k stars on GitHub, and it not only a quantization method but also:  - tensor library for machine learning (a true alternative to pytorch written C and C++)\n- LLM inference engine mostly targeting CPUs\n- binary file format for storing quantized models",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
