    {
      "id": "_9YzIxXsummh7sLCg9xPm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3926.9887489927287,
      "y": 4507.913493188076,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "jw92B3wPlbMwjMWXUx6OZ",
        "m4WDINvB68uup37iqzdZz",
        "iHk_U8sIVPZxHA8DDCQ7W",
        "jlfZ8rE6zfya4Cjd_rDFa"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVf8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 765194938,
      "version": 7555,
      "versionNonce": 1677925840,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789421757275,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Quantization reduces this memory footprint by representing groups of weights using:\n    - a small set of quantized integer values;\n    - additional metadata (scale factors and sometimes offsets).\n\nSo to reduce memory footprint instead of storing the FP16 weight we stored the quantize INT weights\nand recistruct using the scale at inference time.\n\nllama.cpp performs the dequantization operation operation block-wise: weights are divided into small\nblocks, and each block shares quantization parameters. The original legacy formats (Q4_0, Q5_0, Q4_1,\nQ5_1) introduced the two fundamental approaches: type-0 and type-1 quantization.\n\n    - type 0, symmetric -> S\n    - type 1, asymmetric -> (S, a)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFor the legacy GGML/GGUF quantization formats (Q4_0, Q4_1, Q5_0, Q5_1), a block is typically 32\nweights. Using block quantization, block size is a nob, bigger means less quantization constants (save\nspace) but loss granularity. \nStill for a block size 32 and a model of 16B need 2B just for the quantizatoin constants on top of the\nweights. K solves this.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Quantization reduces this memory footprint by representing groups of weights using:\n    - a small set of quantized integer values;\n    - additional metadata (scale factors and sometimes offsets).\n\nSo to reduce memory footprint instead of storing the FP16 weight we stored the quantize INT weights and recistruct using the scale at inference time.\n\nllama.cpp performs the dequantization operation operation block-wise: weights are divided into small blocks, and each block shares quantization parameters. The original legacy formats (Q4_0, Q5_0, Q4_1, Q5_1) introduced the two fundamental approaches: type-0 and type-1 quantization.\n\n    - type 0, symmetric -> S\n    - type 1, asymmetric -> (S, a)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFor the legacy GGML/GGUF quantization formats (Q4_0, Q4_1, Q5_0, Q5_1), a block is typically 32 weights. Using block quantization, block size is a nob, bigger means less quantization constants (save space) but loss granularity. \nStill for a block size 32 and a model of 16B need 2B just for the quantizatoin constants on top of the weights. K solves this.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
