    },
    {
      "id": "k0FQYeSaK05BGgvU11FNY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3909.767278575248,
      "y": 5615.933713991245,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "RlVl4cE6uJBrsIdvhDzmU",
        "d7eHMM6gycl3VvKosl7MF",
        "uRMPR8PVS7zQPkG7Tuqsy",
        "jlfZ8rE6zfya4Cjd_rDFa"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVfB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1080753786,
      "version": 7384,
      "versionNonce": 1799781328,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789421757275,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Legacy quantization uses one scale (and optionally one minimum) for a block. K-quantization introduces a\nhierarchical quantization scheme, where the quantization parameters themselves are quantized and\nshared through super-blocks (256). This two level quantization was originally proposed in the QLORA\npaper as Double Quantizaton.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Legacy quantization uses one scale (and optionally one minimum) for a block. K-quantization introduces a hierarchical quantization scheme, where the quantization parameters themselves are quantized and shared through super-blocks (256). This two level quantization was originally proposed in the QLORA paper as Double Quantizaton.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "BQt8QmZiBF9DMREP5lIfL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3522.7489823453,
      "y": 6275.316661004838,
      "width": 241.2720947265625,
      "height": 41.4,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "kK-DEzfDRC-pRKqC4Rrcp",
        "8oLhUx6jpSHUq0JiUX0DV",
        "EoCg6xIYIIUOl6fQe29bl",
        "jlfZ8rE6zfya4Cjd_rDFa"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVfC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1715019578,
      "version": 660,
      "versionNonce": 1039299102,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789589059984,
      "link": "https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/4773",
      "locked": false,
      "text": "I Quantizatoins",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "I Quantizatoins",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "_4S6a9Lk1wtQEcBGsbVCm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3845.8808106534057,
      "y": 6385.241088469166,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 4450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "KQuKyaq0-9OsI-fkgLIyD",
        "E1jUbdy93nbepZzzYjHM-",
        "EoCg6xIYIIUOl6fQe29bl",
        "jlfZ8rE6zfya4Cjd_rDFa"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVfCV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 386213882,
      "version": 10184,
      "versionNonce": 1276495824,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789421757275,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Before I-quants, llama.cpp and all the other quantization approaches we've seen had scalar quantization.\nScalar quantization assumes that each weight is independent and only cares about the range of\nindividual values. The authors of the QuIP# paper noticed that scalar quantization is suboptimal as it\nignores the shape of the source distribution.\n\nTo overcome this we need to stop seeing weights as individal values and start looking at them as\nvectors of weights in an N dimensional space.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWe can than create a set of reference vectors (which are the equivalent of the integer bins in scalar\nquantization). Each is stored in a codebook which associate it to a a binary code.\n\n\n\n   \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nQuantizing the weight vector means\n    1) applying the quantization formula to each weight\n    2)finding it's nearest reference vector in the codebook.\nThis is called k-bit Vector Compression!\n\nwhere:\n\n    - k = bits per dimension\n    - d = vector dimension\n\na vector is quantized to one of 2^kd vectors in R^d that form a codebook\n\nTo minimize this loss we need to have more reference vectors in the codebook and more dimensions, in\nthis way the codebook can approximate the true distribution better.\n\n\nHow many vectors and with what dimension?\nLet's focus on IQ2_XXS from PR #4773, because that is the first I-quant introduced.\n\nIQ2 style quantization works on 8-dimensional vectors. The goal is therefore to represent these 8\nvalues: \n\n                            w = [−0.80, 0.35, −1.10, 0.20, 0.95, −0.50, 0.15, 0.70]\n\nwith roughly: 8×2=16 bits\n\nNow... if we had normal 2-bit scalar quantization, then each weight can assume 2^2=4 scalar levels.\n\n                                            code      value\n                                            \n                                            00   →   -1.0\n                                            01   →   -0.33\n                                            10   →    0.33\n                                            11   →    1.0\n\nFor 8 dimensions: 4^8 possible combinations and 65536 vectors.\nA naive VQ codebook containing every possible scalar-quantized combination would therefore require:\n65536 × 8 numbers.\n\n                                                         8 dimensions\n                                        \n                                        Code 0    [x x x x x x x x]\n                                        Code 1    [x x x x x x x x]\n                                        Code 2    [x x x x x x x x]\n                                        ...\n                                        Code 65535[x x x x x x x x]\n\n\nHow to avoid this?\n\n\nThe first trick: separate magnitude from sign\n\nThe PR first records which values are negative and converts the working copy to magnitudes.\n\n                        magnitudes\n                        [0.80, 0.35, 1.10, 0.20, 0.95, 0.50, 0.15, 0.70]\n                        \n                        sign pattern\n                        [ -,    +,    -,    +,    +,    -,    +,    + ]\n\n\nWe can write this as:\n\n                w = s * a \n\nwhere:\n\n    - M = absolute values (magnitudes)\n    - S = signs\n    - * = element-wise multiplication\n\nSo we do not need a separate table entry for every sign variation of the same magnitude pattern. You\nstore that template once, then encode the signs separately. This already eliminates enormous\nduplication.\n\n\nThe second trick: E8 lattice\nThe E8 has a property called parity:\n\n\n\nwhich allowing us to encode the signs with 7 bits instead of 8. \nAccording to this property flipping any (nonzero) even number of signs of an element in D̂8 , yields\nanother distinct element in D̂8 . This means that if |D̂8 | denotes the set of elementwise absolute\nvalues of entries in D̂8 , then each element of D̂8 can be expressed (uniquely) as the elementwise\nproduct of an entry s ∈ |D̂8 | and a sign vector of appropriate parity.\n\nExample:\n\n                    Magnitude vector: a = [1,3,1,5,3,1,5,3]\n                    \n                    Sign vector: s = [+,-,+,+,-,+,-,+]\n                    \n                    Multiply: a ⊙ s = [+1,-3,+1,+5,-3,+1,-5,+3]\n\n\nSo, if we start from some “source codebook” of absolute entries S ⊂ |D̂8 |, this property forces the\nsum of its coordinates to be even. Thus, given s ∈ S and 7 out of the 8 sign flips, we can infer the\nlast one from the parity of the 7 sign flips and s.\n\n            +   -   +   +   -  +   -  sum to 4 => in lattice\n            +   -   +   +   -  +   +  sum to 5 => NOT in lattice\n\nThis is why 7 bits are enough. \n\n\nWait... but the real quantized weights will naturally have arbitrary signs. Sometimes I will have 5 positive\nand 3 negative values. If the codebook only accepts even positives then this exact sign pattern is\nimpossible.\n\nIn GGUF, in order to fall back into a redular E8 vector we actually only need to change one sign! Any\ninvalid pattern is only one sign flip away from a valid pattern. How do we choose the weight that will\nabsorb the error?\n\nIn GGUf the candidate is the quant with minimum i * w^2, where w is the model weight and i is the\nimportance of this weight derived from embedding statistics obtained in a calibration run (the\n\"importance matrix\"  -> same as AWQ).\n\nFor a 2-bit quantization one has at most 256 slots available with the 8 bits used for the magnitueds,\nso one needs to select a subset of the possible E8 lattice points. GGUF adopt a data driven approach.\n\nTo pick the 256 grid points, perform quantization using all possible E8 lattice points on a bunch of\nmodels. For N allowed quant values there are N^8 grid points. Count how many times each of the points\noccurred in the quantized models\nSelect the 256 points such that:\n    a) The total count of selected points is maximized and \n    b) The maximum distance of not selected points to the closest selected point is minimized\n\n\nRecall that GGUF utilizes block scaling. Specifivally a block of 32 weight.\n\nFor one vector of 8 weight we use 16bit, each vector has a spare bit, which gives us 4 spare bits in the\nblock.\n\nThey use this for a 4-bit scale, so a \"true\" 2-bit quantization.\n\n\n\n\nThis does not take into account the scale factor. But becuase I-quants share the same scale for 256\nvectors the overhead is minimal. All of this was for IQ2 quantization. There are many more paradigmns:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Before I-quants, llama.cpp and all the other quantization approaches we've seen had scalar quantization.\nScalar quantization assumes that each weight is independent and only cares about the range of individual values. The authors of the QuIP# paper noticed that scalar quantization is suboptimal as it ignores the shape of the source distribution.\n\nTo overcome this we need to stop seeing weights as individal values and start looking at them as vectors of weights in an N dimensional space.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWe can than create a set of reference vectors (which are the equivalent of the integer bins in scalar quantization). Each is stored in a codebook which associate it to a a binary code.\n\n\n\n   \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nQuantizing the weight vector means\n    1) applying the quantization formula to each weight\n    2)finding it's nearest reference vector in the codebook.\nThis is called k-bit Vector Compression!\n\nwhere:\n\n    - k = bits per dimension\n    - d = vector dimension\n\na vector is quantized to one of 2^kd vectors in R^d that form a codebook\n\nTo minimize this loss we need to have more reference vectors in the codebook and more dimensions, in this way the codebook can approximate the true distribution better.\n\n\nHow many vectors and with what dimension?\nLet's focus on IQ2_XXS from PR #4773, because that is the first I-quant introduced.\n\nIQ2 style quantization works on 8-dimensional vectors. The goal is therefore to represent these 8 values: \n\n                            w = [−0.80, 0.35, −1.10, 0.20, 0.95, −0.50, 0.15, 0.70]\n\nwith roughly: 8×2=16 bits\n\nNow... if we had normal 2-bit scalar quantization, then each weight can assume 2^2=4 scalar levels.\n\n                                            code      value\n                                            \n                                            00   →   -1.0\n                                            01   →   -0.33\n                                            10   →    0.33\n                                            11   →    1.0\n\nFor 8 dimensions: 4^8 possible combinations and 65536 vectors.\nA naive VQ codebook containing every possible scalar-quantized combination would therefore require: 65536 × 8 numbers.\n\n                                                         8 dimensions\n                                        \n                                        Code 0    [x x x x x x x x]\n                                        Code 1    [x x x x x x x x]\n                                        Code 2    [x x x x x x x x]\n                                        ...\n                                        Code 65535[x x x x x x x x]\n\n\nHow to avoid this?\n\n\nThe first trick: separate magnitude from sign\n\nThe PR first records which values are negative and converts the working copy to magnitudes.\n\n                        magnitudes\n                        [0.80, 0.35, 1.10, 0.20, 0.95, 0.50, 0.15, 0.70]\n                        \n                        sign pattern\n                        [ -,    +,    -,    +,    +,    -,    +,    + ]\n\n\nWe can write this as:\n\n                w = s * a \n\nwhere:\n\n    - M = absolute values (magnitudes)\n    - S = signs\n    - * = element-wise multiplication\n\nSo we do not need a separate table entry for every sign variation of the same magnitude pattern. You store that template once, then encode the signs separately. This already eliminates enormous duplication.\n\n\nThe second trick: E8 lattice\nThe E8 has a property called parity:\n\n\n\nwhich allowing us to encode the signs with 7 bits instead of 8. \nAccording to this property flipping any (nonzero) even number of signs of an element in D̂8 , yields another distinct element in D̂8 . This means that if |D̂8 | denotes the set of elementwise absolute values of entries in D̂8 , then each element of D̂8 can be expressed (uniquely) as the elementwise product of an entry s ∈ |D̂8 | and a sign vector of appropriate parity.\n\nExample:\n\n                    Magnitude vector: a = [1,3,1,5,3,1,5,3]\n                    \n                    Sign vector: s = [+,-,+,+,-,+,-,+]\n                    \n                    Multiply: a ⊙ s = [+1,-3,+1,+5,-3,+1,-5,+3]\n\n\nSo, if we start from some “source codebook” of absolute entries S ⊂ |D̂8 |, this property forces the sum of its coordinates to be even. Thus, given s ∈ S and 7 out of the 8 sign flips, we can infer the last one from the parity of the 7 sign flips and s.\n\n            +   -   +   +   -  +   -  sum to 4 => in lattice\n            +   -   +   +   -  +   +  sum to 5 => NOT in lattice\n\nThis is why 7 bits are enough. \n\n\nWait... but the real quantized weights will naturally have arbitrary signs. Sometimes I will have 5 positive and 3 negative values. If the codebook only accepts even positives then this exact sign pattern is impossible.\n\nIn GGUF, in order to fall back into a redular E8 vector we actually only need to change one sign! Any invalid pattern is only one sign flip away from a valid pattern. How do we choose the weight that will absorb the error?\n\nIn GGUf the candidate is the quant with minimum i * w^2, where w is the model weight and i is the importance of this weight derived from embedding statistics obtained in a calibration run (the \"importance matrix\"  -> same as AWQ).\n\nFor a 2-bit quantization one has at most 256 slots available with the 8 bits used for the magnitueds, so one needs to select a subset of the possible E8 lattice points. GGUF adopt a data driven approach.\n\nTo pick the 256 grid points, perform quantization using all possible E8 lattice points on a bunch of models. For N allowed quant values there are N^8 grid points. Count how many times each of the points occurred in the quantized models\nSelect the 256 points such that:\n    a) The total count of selected points is maximized and \n    b) The maximum distance of not selected points to the closest selected point is minimized\n\n\nRecall that GGUF utilizes block scaling. Specifivally a block of 32 weight.\n\nFor one vector of 8 weight we use 16bit, each vector has a spare bit, which gives us 4 spare bits in the block.\n\nThey use this for a 4-bit scale, so a \"true\" 2-bit quantization.\n\n\n\n\nThis does not take into account the scale factor. But becuase I-quants share the same scale for 256 vectors the overhead is minimal. All of this was for IQ2 quantization. There are many more paradigmns:",
