    {
      "id": "vGpzidmrJd7IKpbhh3ikT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4462.566023794796,
      "y": 9663.386111374139,
      "width": 531.6157090928818,
      "height": 320,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#e03131",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "EoCg6xIYIIUOl6fQe29bl",
        "jlfZ8rE6zfya4Cjd_rDFa"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bVfW",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2057025338,
      "version": 647,
      "versionNonce": 228278736,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789421757275,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A lattice is a regular arrangement of points in space. In 1 dimension:\n\n<----|----|----|----|---->\n    -2   -1    0    1    2\n\n\nQuantization is basically: given a vector \\(w\\), find the closest\nrepresentable point. A lattice gives us a structured set of possible\npoints.\n\nE8 is a lattice in 8-dimensional space which contains special 8D\npoints arranged in a way that gives extremely efficient packing. \n\nQuIP# uses vector quantization to take advantage of the ball-\nshaped sub-Gaussian distribution that incoherent weights possess.\nIn high dimensions, we can imagine this as a N-dimensional sphere.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A lattice is a regular arrangement of points in space. In 1 dimension:\n\n<----|----|----|----|---->\n    -2   -1    0    1    2\n\n\nQuantization is basically: given a vector \\(w\\), find the closest representable point. A lattice gives us a structured set of possible points.\n\nE8 is a lattice in 8-dimensional space which contains special 8D points arranged in a way that gives extremely efficient packing. \n\nQuIP# uses vector quantization to take advantage of the ball-shaped sub-Gaussian distribution that incoherent weights possess. In high dimensions, we can imagine this as a N-dimensional sphere.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
