      "updated": 1789588462877,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Calibration",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Calibration",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "xJhOTvHXiEGf1UeIfk4t3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -13143.526014410829,
      "y": 9987.442517634961,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "A_-yWF6z4o8W7-JHRYn12",
        "2BSV0B04mK-tRxLppZJv1"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bgO",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 742891814,
      "version": 4067,
      "versionNonce": 2022086814,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789588462877,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "It is easy to imagine how to use the quantization formulas for the W matrix because its values are\nfixed.\nTo quantize X we do not have a precomputed scale and zero point, but it can be quantized on the fly,\nusing a process called dynamic quantization, ot we can use another approach called calibration.\nIf for the quantization of the activation we have an option, for dequantize the output, calibration is\nmandatory.\n\nCalibration is the process of finding alpha and beta. \n\nA simple approach would be taking the minimum ancd maximum values in the model weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe problem is that very sensitive to outliers: there might be gaps between the alpha and the central\nvalues, which leads to wasting a lot of space in the quantized  space. \n\nWe have to make the most from the few bits we have!\n\nTo minimze this waste we could ignore the outliers, e.g. using percentiles.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe trade off is that we are loosing accuracy because all the outliers to the left and right will be\nclamped to q_min and q_max.\n\nThe final goal is to have the two distributinos to be as similar as possible. i.e. only way to minimize this\nis trying different therholds and measure the difference between the input and output distribution.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "It is easy to imagine how to use the quantization formulas for the W matrix because its values are fixed.\nTo quantize X we do not have a precomputed scale and zero point, but it can be quantized on the fly, using a process called dynamic quantization, ot we can use another approach called calibration.\nIf for the quantization of the activation we have an option, for dequantize the output, calibration is mandatory.\n\nCalibration is the process of finding alpha and beta. \n\nA simple approach would be taking the minimum ancd maximum values in the model weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe problem is that very sensitive to outliers: there might be gaps between the alpha and the central values, which leads to wasting a lot of space in the quantized  space. \n\nWe have to make the most from the few bits we have!\n\nTo minimze this waste we could ignore the outliers, e.g. using percentiles.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe trade off is that we are loosing accuracy because all the outliers to the left and right will be clamped to q_min and q_max.\n\nThe final goal is to have the two distributinos to be as similar as possible. i.e. only way to minimize this is trying different therholds and measure the difference between the input and output distribution.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
