      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bjI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1054614886,
      "version": 763,
      "versionNonce": 1472738910,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "C2LH3nJZTPzNRClt3CHji",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1789588462880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Internally, integers are represented as sums of\npowers of two, so multiplying or dividing by 2^n has a\nvery simple hardware interpretation: shift the bits.\n\nIf you multiply 12 by 2, you can just shift left by\none bit:\n\n00001100  →  00011000\n      12               24\n\nSimilarly, dividing by 2^n is basically a right shift.\n\nFor a multiplication by 10there is no single binary\nshift that does that...",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Internally, integers are represented as sums of powers of two, so multiplying or dividing by 2^n has a very simple hardware interpretation: shift the bits.\n\nIf you multiply 12 by 2, you can just shift left by one bit:\n\n00001100  →  00011000\n      12               24\n\nSimilarly, dividing by 2^n is basically a right shift.\n\nFor a multiplication by 10there is no single binary shift that does that...",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
